      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Italian publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends on which one you aim at, not on how many editors you email.</p>

            <p>The national newspapers do not run guest posts. The verticals and digital-native magazines do. The business and legal sites take practitioner analysis. The smaller blogs are wide open. The list of 100 is below, grouped roughly by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>National newspapers &amp; wires</td><td>Repubblica, Corriere, La Stampa, Il Sole 24 Ore, ANSA, AGI</td><td>Not guest posts. An op-ed (intervento) or expert commentary on a live story</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Verticals &amp; digital magazines</td><td>Wired.it, Focus, Vanity Fair, Vogue.it, DiLei, GreenMe, TuttoAndroid, La Cucina Italiana</td><td>Freelance and contributor pitches in Italian; how-tos, analysis, buying guides with a fresh angle</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Business, finance &amp; legal</td><td>Affari Italiani, Money.it, QuiFinanza, Investire Oggi, La Legge per Tutti, Italia Oggi</td><td>Practitioner analysis, original data, market explainers; strict on disclosure for YMYL topics</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Niche &amp; smaller blogs</td><td>BlogSicilia, Benessereblog, Bebeblog, Giardinaggio.it, ItalianBark, Marketing Italia</td><td>Open to contributions; practical guides, local angles, first-hand experience</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> the Italian market is smaller, so a placement on a mid-DR vertical read by your buyers is worth more than a link buried in a national daily's archive. Judge relevance and the section first, DR second.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 Italian sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; shut-down and absorbed brands were swapped for live equivalents. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr>
                </thead>
