                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for Italian editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Write to the editor in Italian.</strong> A pitch in English marks you as someone who will file English copy. Send it in the language the piece will run in.</li>
              <li><strong>Lead with the Italian angle.</strong> Italian data, an Italian case, or how a trend plays out in the Italian market, in the first two lines.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section (rubrica), not the masthead.</strong> "Per la vostra sezione tecnologia" shows you read the site and know where the piece fits.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring something a staff writer cannot get.</strong> Proprietary data, a survey of Italian users, first-hand operator experience, an interview.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the format to the tier.</strong> Op-ed desks want a tight argument. Verticals want a structured how-to. Blogs want first-hand detail.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any client or brand tie</strong> up front. Italian editors check, and a hidden conflict ends the relationship.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would this editor run the piece if you had nothing to sell? If yes, pitch it. If it only exists to place a link, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Send an auto-translated article. Italian editors reject it on the first paragraph.</li>
              <li>Pitch Repubblica or Corriere a "guest post." That format does not exist there; you burn the contact.</li>
              <li>Use the same pitch for a national title and a niche blog. The angle has to change.</li>
              <li>Put exact-match anchors ("miglior CRM") on links back to your product or store.</li>
              <li>Ignore disclosure rules on finance, health and legal topics. One buried conflict and the piece is pulled.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a handful of placements on Italian sites your buyers actually read, written by a native speaker, will move rankings and revenue more than a long list of high-DR links nobody in the market sees. Use this list to find your handful.</p></div>
